# Build context exclusions.
#
# Without this file, `docker build` uploaded ~480 MB of context (459 MB
# frontend/node_modules + 16 MB .git + the local frontend/dist) on every
# run. Worse than the size: Stage 1 does `COPY frontend/package*.json` ->
# `npm ci` -> `COPY frontend/ ./`, so a host node_modules directory in the
# context OVERWRITES the linux/arm64 dependencies npm ci just installed
# with whatever the host has (darwin binaries on a Mac, plus any iCloud
# " 2" conflict copies). CI never hit this because a fresh checkout has no
# node_modules; a local `docker build` did, silently.
.git
.gitignore
**/node_modules

# Nothing in the Dockerfile copies from deploy/, and that directory holds the
# .env with MODULAB_MASTER_KEY, the database and Valkey passwords and the
# Cloudflare token. Excluding it keeps them out of the build context
# entirely rather than relying on no COPY ever reaching for them.
deploy/

# Build outputs. Both are produced inside the image by Stage 1
# (`npm run build`), so a stale local copy must never leak into the build.
frontend/dist
backend/internal/webui/dist

# Never needed at build time, and .env in particular must not end up in an
# image layer.
#
# The "**/" matters: .dockerignore patterns are matched against paths
# relative to the build-context root, so a bare ".env" excludes only the one
# at the top. frontend/.env is a real file (see frontend/.env.example, and
# frontend/.gitignore lists it), it is pulled in by "COPY frontend/ ./", and
# Vite inlines every VITE_-prefixed value from it straight into the bundle -
# which is then embedded into the binary and served to every browser. Same
# class of local-only leak the node_modules note above describes.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/.DS_Store
cosign.key
cosign.pub
